---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

/* Kept honest on purpose: the app is on a closed test track, not in the store.
   The day it is public, the badge and this line change together. */
const status = 'In closed testing on Google Play';
const updated = 'July 28, 2026';

/* Real captures from the release build, not mockups. Each one sits beside the
   thing it shows: a strip of every screen at the top told nobody anything,
   because a screenshot with no sentence next to it is decoration. */
const updatedShots = 'August 2, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Munchview — say how long you have, and watch"
  description="Munchview starts a good YouTube video that fits the time you have — a lunch break, a study session, something on in the workshop, winding down. No feed, no browsing. Android, in closed testing."
>
  <SiteChrome />
  <div class="sky" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="s1"></span><span class="s2"></span><span class="s3"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <main id="main" class="product">
    <span class="eyebrow">Munchview</span>
    <h1>Say how long you have.<br /><span class="serif">Then just watch.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every app in this category opens by asking what you are in the mood for.
      Munchview is built on the belief that the asking is the problem. Tell it
      how long you have got — ten minutes, forty, whatever you set — and
      something worth watching is already playing.
    </p>
    <p class="status"><span class="badge testing">{status}</span></p>

    <figure class="hero-shot">
      <img
        src="/munchview/shots/hero-home.webp"
        alt="The Munchview home screen: the question “How long have you got?”, a custom length of about twenty minutes, and one large amber Press to watch button."
        width="540"
        height="1200"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>One question, one button. That is the whole opening screen.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="how">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2 id="how">Three seconds, start to watching</h2>
        <p>
          The whole app was built against a stopwatch. Open it, press one
          button, and a video is running. There is no feed to fall into, no
          autoplay rabbit hole, and nothing to scroll before you have chosen
          anything.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><b>Open it.</b> One question, one button.</li>
          <li><b>Say how long.</b> A preset, or any length you like.</li>
          <li><b>Watch.</b> It is already playing.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <figure class="feature-shot">
        <img
          src="/munchview/shots/player-feed.webp"
          alt="A nature documentary playing at the top of the Munchview player, with a Related to this list of further videos underneath it."
          width="540"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Playing immediately — and what to watch next, already lined up.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <figure class="band">
      <img
        src="/munchview/shots/fullscreen.webp"
        alt="A wildlife documentary filling an Android phone held sideways, with no controls or chrome over the picture at all."
        width="1200"
        height="540"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Turn the phone and the picture takes everything. A few seconds later the
        controls fade — even the progress line — and only the video is left.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="feature reverse" aria-labelledby="shorts">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2 id="shorts">A minute, not an evening</h2>
        <p>
          Some breaks are ninety seconds long. Shorts are here too, chosen by the
          same taste as everything else — swipe up for the next one, down for the
          last, sideways to leave. Autoplay is a switch, not a decision made for
          you.
        </p>
        <p>
          The controls are the ones you already know from the full player: skip
          five seconds either way, a scrub bar, like and not-for-me. One tap
          brings them up, one tap takes them away.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="feature-shot">
        <img
          src="/munchview/shots/shorts.webp"
          alt="A short vertical video playing in Munchview with the amber play control in the centre and like, not-for-me and settings buttons stacked down the right edge."
          width="540"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Vertical clips, the same controls, no separate app to learn.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="picks">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2 id="picks">It learns, without watching you</h2>
        <p>
          What it plays is ranked from what you have told it: the categories you
          chose, the length you asked for, the channels you liked, the ones you
          turned down, and what you have already been shown so the same few
          videos do not come round again. All of that is worked out on the phone.
        </p>
        <ul class="points">
          <li>
            <b>Your country's categories.</b> The list comes from YouTube for
            wherever you are, so it offers what people there actually watch.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Type a show, keep it as a tag.</b> Write the name of something you
            like and it joins the mix, matched to the length you asked for.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Pick up where you stopped.</b> Everything you started keeps its
            place, with an amber line on the picture showing how far you got.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Paste any link.</b> YouTube, Vimeo, Dailymotion, Twitch and the
            rest all play inside the app. Nothing is handed off to a browser.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="feature-shot">
        <img
          src="/munchview/shots/discover.webp"
          alt="The Munchview home screen scrolled down: a Continue watching row with time remaining, a row of Shorts, category chips, and a box for pasting any video link."
          width="540"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Continue watching, Shorts, your categories, and a box for any link.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="feature reverse" aria-labelledby="youtube">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <h2 id="youtube">Your YouTube likes — kept separate</h2>
        <p>
          Connect your YouTube account and the videos you liked there come in as
          <em>their own list</em>. They are never mixed into Munchview's own
          likes, because the recommendations are built from what you like
          <em>here</em> — and you should always be able to tell which app thought
          what.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is read-only. Munchview never likes, subscribes, comments or changes
          anything on your account, and it cannot read your YouTube watch
          history — no app can, since YouTube stopped providing it. Disconnect
          removes the imported list from the phone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="feature-shot wide-shot">
        <img
          src="/munchview/shots/youtube.webp"
          alt="The Connect YouTube button in Munchview's settings, in YouTube red, above an explanation that the imported likes stay a separate list."
          width="540"
          height="250"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>One button in Settings. Read-only, and reversible.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="whenever">
      <h2 id="whenever">Not only at mealtimes</h2>
      <p>
        It started as something to watch while you eat, and it outgrew that. A
        study break, something on in the workshop while your hands are busy, a
        match in the background, winding down before sleep — the question is
        always the same one, and so is the answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="privacy">
      <h2 id="privacy">No ads, no tracking, no feed</h2>
      <p>
        Munchview is not funded by advertising, so it is not built to watch you.
        As a guest it collects nothing at all: what you like, skip and save
        stays on the phone. Signing in with Google is optional and does exactly
        one thing — carries that to a second phone. It asks Google only who you
        are; connecting YouTube is a separate choice you make yourself, and it
        only ever reads.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/munchview/privacy">Read the full privacy policy<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp;
        <a href="/munchview/delete-account">Delete your account<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="status">
      <h2 id="status">Where it is right now</h2>
      <p>
        Munchview is on a closed test track on Google Play. It is a finished,
        installable app — the test track exists so it is used on real phones by
        real people before it goes public, which is also what Google asks of a
        new developer account.
      </p>
      <p>
        Want to be one of the testers? Write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@deftday.com?subject=Munchview%20tester">hello@deftday.com</a>
        with the Google account you use on your Android phone, and we will add
        you to the track.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated} · Screens captured {updatedShots}</p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .product {
    max-width: 78ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 160px var(--gutter) 40px;
  }
  .product h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.4vw, 1.15rem);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .status {
    margin-top: 26px;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: 56px;
    padding-top: 34px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .block h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .block p {
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .steps,
  .points {
    color: var(--muted);
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .steps b,
  .points b {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .points {
    list-style: none;
    padding-left: 0;
  }
  .points li {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  .points li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.62em;
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--grad);
  }
  .updated {
    margin-top: 22px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Screens.
     Each capture sits beside the words it belongs to. The old page put all
     seven in one scrolling strip at the top, which asked the reader to work out
     for themselves what they were looking at — a picture with no sentence next
     to it is decoration, not an explanation.

     The page column is text-width, so these rows are allowed to break out of it
     on a wide screen and come back to a single column on a phone. */
  .product {
    max-width: 78ch;
  }
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: #12100e;
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The opening screen, sized so it reads as a phone rather than a poster. */
  .hero-shot {
    margin: 44px auto 0;
    max-width: 280px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* A feature: words on one side, the screen that proves them on the other. */
  .feature {
    display: grid;
    gap: 30px;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 64px;
    padding-top: 38px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .feature h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .feature p {
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .feature-shot {
    max-width: 260px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .feature-shot.wide-shot {
    max-width: 420px;
  }

  /* Wide enough for two columns: alternate the side the picture lands on, so
     the eye is not dragged down one rail. */
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .feature {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr minmax(0, 300px);
      gap: 56px;
    }
    .feature.reverse {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 300px) 1fr;
    }
    .feature.reverse .feature-text {
      order: 2;
    }
    .feature.reverse .feature-shot {
      order: 1;
    }
    .feature-shot {
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  /* The landscape capture earns the full width — it is the one screen where the
     whole point is that nothing else is on it. */
  .band {
    margin-top: 64px;
    padding-top: 38px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .band img {
    border-radius: 16px;
  }
  .band figcaption {
    margin-inline: auto;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }
  @media (min-width: 1100px) {
    .band {
      width: min(1100px, 92vw);
      margin-inline: calc(50% - min(550px, 46vw));
    }
  }
</style>
